# plugin-a-reach-sibling.layer-a
# Untrusted plugin "A" reaching plugin B's directory, under host attenuation
# (C4, least-privilege delegation enforced below the host).
#
# Intent: this plugin writes to /plugins/B/config.json -- a path the HOST itself
# is fully authorized to write, because the host holds broad authority over the
# whole plugin tree, Filesystem.Write{/plugins/*}. The point of C4 is that the
# host does NOT hand the plugin its own broad authority. It runs the plugin under
# a narrowed grant -- Filesystem.Write confined to /plugins/A/ -- so even though
# the host could write to /plugins/B, the scoped plugin cannot. The plugin is
# honest (the authored EffectDecl pins the refinement to the call argument, so it
# admits cleanly); the denial is not because the plugin misbehaved at admission
# but because the delegated slice does not cover /plugins/B. Least privilege is
# enforced by the grant the runtime applies, not by the plugin's good behavior.
@v=1 root=writeApp
strT PRM String
bytesT PRM Bytes
writeFx EFC "Filesystem.Write" [strT]
writeT FNT [strT bytesT] intT
writeFn FN "strand-builtin:Fs.Write" writeT [writeFx] "writeFx:0"
siblingPath STR "/plugins/B/config.json"
payload BYT "7b22636f6e666967223a317d"
writeDecl EFD writeFx [siblingPath]
writeApp APP writeFn [siblingPath payload] [] [writeDecl]
